        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Our waste, our responsibility"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "A dustbin full of plastic, a hole in the ozone layer: the environment faces problems of human making. We can reduce them."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "CFCs",
          "biodegradable",
          "composting",
          "everyday habits",
          "non-biodegradable",
          "ozone",
          "reduce, reuse, recycle",
          "single-use plastic"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "Ozone (O3) in the stratosphere absorbs harmful ultraviolet radiation from the sun.\nChlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) used in refrigerators and aerosols destroy ozone. In 1987 UNEP asked countries to freeze CFC production.\nWaste is biodegradable if bacteria and other organisms can break it down, like vegetable peel, and non-biodegradable if not, like plastics and glass.\nNon-biodegradable waste stays for long, harms animals and may enter food chains.\nWaste management: reduce, reuse and recycle. Segregate wet and dry waste at home.\nComposting makes manure from biodegradable waste. Landfills and incineration have problems of their own.\nUse cloth bags, avoid single-use plastic and dispose of waste properly.\nAwareness and everyday habits protect the environment."
        }
      },
